<code>extended.mod.json</code> is an optional file alongside <code>mod.json</code>. Use it to define player-facing settings, organize them into groups, add actions and links, and store the enabled state.

== Main fields ==

* <code>schemaVersion</code> is currently <code>1</code>.
* <code>enabled</code> stores whether the mod is switched on.
* <code>targets</code> selects the process that loads the mod. It does not replicate changes over the network.
* <code>settings</code> stores mod-specific values.
* <code>groups</code> organizes settings in the Modloader. Ungrouped settings appear in a default group.
* <code>launcher</code> can preserve the origin of an imported EML mod.

== Example ==

<pre>
{
  "$schema": "https://bonsaibauer.github.io/shroudforge/schemas/extended.mod.schema.json",
  "schemaVersion": 1,
  "enabled": false,
  "targets": ["client"],
  "settings": {
    "greeting": {
      "value": "Hello from my mod",
      "label": "Greeting",
      "control": "text"
    }
  },
  "groups": [
    {
      "label": "Messages",
      "settings": ["greeting"]
    }
  ]
}
</pre>

The setting key is <code>greeting</code>. Lua code reads the value by using that exact key with <code>shroudforge.settings.get</code>. A group makes the setting visible in the Modloader.

The [https://bonsaibauer.github.io/shroudforge/schemas/extended.mod.schema.json canonical extended manifest schema] defines the structure and data types. The [https://bonsaibauer.github.io/shroudforge/ ShroudForge documentation] includes a live manifest builder.

